# <%= current_page.data.title %>

This is our agreement about how we work together. It is not a rulebook. It is a
starting point for being clear, kind, and dependable when the work is easy and
when it is not.

## Working together

We turn cameras on by default in team meetings because it makes conversation
easier.

We use our Slack status and calendars to make availability visible.
This helps people plan work without having to guess whether someone is 
available. 

Nobody is expected to reply outside their working hours or while 
their status says they are away.

We make room for everyone to contribute. Ask questions, say when something is
unclear and challenge ideas to make things better for everyone.

When a meeting or huddle produces a decision, action or useful learning, we
write it down where the people affected can find it.

## Slack

Use `#integration-hub-team` for team discussion, coordination, and sharing what we
are learning.

Use `#ask-integration-hub` for questions and requests from people
using, or planning to use, our services.

Slack is asynchronous. If you need an answer by a particular time, say when and
explain why.

We do our best to acknowledge customer questions during working hours and say
what will happen next. The Integration Hub is not yet a live service, so this
expectation will develop with our customers' needs.

Keep follow-up discussion in a thread when it helps the channel remain easy to
scan. Do not put passwords, private keys, file contents or other sensitive data
in Slack.

## GitHub

GitHub is central to our work. We store our code here, hold our product backlog 
here, and run our CI/CD pipelines here.

GitHub is where we keep work that needs an owner, a decision or a useful
history. Pull requests should explain the outcome they change and how the
change was checked. Link the issue, architecture decision record or discussion
that explains why the work matters.

When something is blocked, say so. When it is resolved, close the loop where
people are already following the work. Nobody should need to reconstruct a
decision from half a dozen old Slack threads and a meeting they did not attend.

